/-!
# VIII04 — endpoint classes and uniqueness on the whole exterior (energy method)

Function classes (explicit; they are the endpoint conditions of the first-order BVP):

* `HorizonC1 R_h b`: some `η > 0` and some `bt`, `C¹` on the two-sided neighbourhood
  `(R_h - η, R_h + η)`, with `bt = b` on `(R_h, R_h + η)` (a genuine `C¹` extension through `R_h`);
* `AFDecay R_h b`: `|b| ≤ C/R`, `|b'| ≤ C/R²` for `R ≥ R_*` (some `C ≥ 0`, `R_* > R_h`) — an ansatz-level decay
  class at the level `a = a1/R + O(R⁻²)` (its equivalence with mere `b → 0` for ODE solutions is not proved);
* `MagClass R_h b := b ∈ C²(I) ∧ HorizonC1 ∧ AFDecay`, `I = (R_h, ∞)`;
* `VClass R_h v := v ∈ C¹(I) ∧ (v has a finite right limit at R_h) ∧ v → 0 at ∞`.

For two solutions of `L b = 2Qj/R³`, `h = b₁ - b₂` and `E = P h h'` (`P = N0/R`):
`E' = P (h')² + V h² ≥ 0` (`energy_hasDerivAt`), `E(R_h⁺) = 0` from the `C¹` extension and
`P(R_h) = 0` (`energy_horizon`), `E(∞) = 0` from `P ≤ R`, `h = O(1/R)`, `h' = O(1/R²)`
(`energy_infinity`). Monotonicity forces `E ≡ 0`, then `E' = 0` and `V > 0` force `h ≡ 0`
(`mag_unique`). The vanishing of the boundary terms is *derived*, never assumed.
`v_unique`: the same `v'` and `v(∞) = 0` fix `v` (no additive gauge left). `v(∞) = 0` is the chosen
normalisation; the finite horizon limit in `VClass` is part of the class but is not used for uniqueness.
